          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Where Small Genuinely Beats Big
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buying advice usually treats a small generator as a compromise. For a large number of households it is simply the better machine, for reasons that never appear on a specification sheet.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>It gets deployed.</strong> A 47-pound machine is out and running in ninety seconds by whoever happens to be home. A 250-pound machine requires the right person, a clear path, and a working battery.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Fuel maths that works.</strong> A 2,200W inverter uses roughly a gallon per 8 hours. Three days is about 9 gallons — two cans in the shed. An 8,000W machine needs 20 to 30, which most households cannot store or buy mid-storm.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>You can live beside it.</strong> 48 to 56 dB against 70 to 76 dB is not an incremental difference over three days and three nights, for you or the neighbours.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>It stores anywhere.</strong> A shelf, a cupboard, the boot of a car. Large machines claim floor space that many garages and all flats simply do not have.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>No electrician required.</strong> Small generators are extension cord machines, which suits renters and anyone unwilling to spend $500 to $1,500 on a transfer switch.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>It does other jobs.</strong> Camping, a caravan, a market stall, power at the end of a garden. A big open-frame machine does none of them.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Set against that, the honest cost of small is that a bad outage in the wrong season exposes the limit — no well water, no air conditioning, and load management you have to think about rather than ignore.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5 -->
        <div id="when-wrong" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When Small Is the Wrong Call
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four situations where the honest recommendation is to spend more and accept the weight:
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>You are on a well.</strong> No mains water means no water at all without the pump, and its 3,000 to 4,000 watt surge is beyond everything here. That single appliance sets your minimum at 7,500 watts and is covered in what size generator runs a well pump.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A sump pump keeps the house dry.</strong> A small unit can run a 1/3 hp pump, but if the basement genuinely floods during the storms that cause outages, the consequences of being marginal are measured in thousands rather than inconvenience.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Losing air conditioning is a health issue.</strong> For elderly or medically vulnerable households in hot climates, cooling is not a comfort item, and no small generator will provide it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Your outages routinely run days rather than hours.</strong> Small machines have small tanks, which means refuelling every 8 to 14 hours including overnight. Across a week that becomes genuinely wearing, and a larger tank starts to earn its weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            If two or more of those apply, look at the full portable range, or at whether a standby unit is the better answer altogether.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6 -->
        <div id="parallel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Running Two Small Generators in Parallel
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most small inverters are parallel-capable: a manufacturer's kit links two identical units so they share a load, and two 2,200W machines deliver roughly 3,600 running watts. It is the upgrade path for a household that is not sure how much generator it needs, and it means a first purchase is never wasted.
          </p>

          <p>
            The advantages are real. You run one machine for a light load and both only when you need the output, which keeps fuel consumption and noise down most of the time. Two smaller units are also easier to carry, store and start than one larger one, and a failure leaves you with half your power rather than none.
          </p>

          <p>
            The honest arithmetic against it is that two paralleled machines usually cost more than one larger machine of the same total output, and you now maintain two engines, two oil changes and two carburetors. If you already know you need 3,700 watts, buy one machine that produces it. Parallel is for uncertainty, not for planned capacity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <div id="battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Engine or Battery?
          </h2>

          <p>
            In the small category, battery power stations are a genuine alternative rather than a curiosity, and the distinction between them turns on one thing: power versus energy.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Distinction That Decides It</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">An engine generator has unlimited energy as long as you feed it. A battery has a fixed budget and needs mains or sun to refill.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Both may deliver 1,800 watts. Only one of them still delivers it on day three of a cloudy winter storm — and only one of them can run inside a flat with no outdoor space at all.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            That makes the choice fairly clean. Choose the battery for a flat or condominium, for medical equipment, for outages measured in hours, and where noise or exhaust rules an engine out entirely. Choose the engine for a house with outdoor space and outages measured in days. Households that can afford both often find them complementary — the battery runs indoors overnight in silence, the engine recharges it and carries the heavy loads by day. The full comparison is in power station versus generator, and the units themselves in our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station testing</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8 -->
        <div id="using" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Using a Small Generator Safely
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Small Does Not Mean Safe Indoors</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              A 2,200-watt generator produces carbon monoxide in lethal concentrations exactly as a 12,000-watt one does. The 20-foot setback from every window, door and vent applies identically, a garage with the door open is not outdoors, and a balcony is not either. Only a battery power station runs inside. Fit battery-powered CO detectors in the house regardless.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Beyond that, small machines are extension cord machines, and the cords matter. Use heavy-gauge outdoor-rated cord — 12 gauge to 50 feet — run through a window with a sealed cord pass rather than a door propped open, which defeats the setback you just observed. Never daisy-chain cords, and keep them out of standing water.
          </p>

          <p>
            The maintenance is the same discipline as any petrol engine, and small machines are no more forgiving. Exercise it under load for 20 minutes a month, store it either full of stabilised fuel run through the carburetor or completely dry, and change the oil after the first 20 to 25 hours and every 50 to 100 thereafter. A gummed carburetor is what stops a stored generator starting, and it does not care how light the machine is.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <PlanTheJob questions={false} />

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best small generator for home use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Honda EU2200i — 48 dB, 47 pounds, and starting reliability that is the industry benchmark. If you need the full essentials package rather than a fridge and lights, the Westinghouse iGen4500 at 3,700 running watts is the most capable machine that still counts as small. For value, the Champion 2500-Watt Inverter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What will a small generator actually run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At 1,800 running watts: a refrigerator, lighting, device charging, a router, and a furnace blower if nothing else is drawing. At 3,000 to 3,700: all of that at once, plus a chest freezer and a small sump pump. What none will do is start a well pump or run central air, which need 7,500 watts or more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a small generator enough for a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most outages, yes — what makes an outage genuinely damaging is a thawing freezer and a house losing heat, and both are within reach of 3,000 watts. It is not enough if you depend on a well pump for water, on a sump pump in heavy rain, or if losing air conditioning is a health issue.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How quiet is a small generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Enclosed inverters run 48 to 56 dB at 23 feet under load — roughly conversation level, often unidentifiable to neighbours. Open-frame inverters run about 58 dB. Conventional open-frame generators of any size run 70 to 76 dB, which is a completely different thing to live beside for three days.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you run two small generators together?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if both are parallel-capable — most small inverters are, with the manufacturer's kit. Two 2,200W units give roughly 3,600 running watts. It is a good hedge against uncertainty, though two paralleled machines usually cost more than one larger one and give you two engines to maintain.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a small generator connect to a house panel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not, and usually it should not. Most lack the 30-amp twist-lock outlet a transfer switch inlet expects, and their output is too low to justify a multi-circuit switch. Small generators are extension cord machines — which is exactly why they suit renters and anyone avoiding an electrician's bill.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much fuel does a small generator use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Far less than expected, and it is one of the strongest arguments for small. A 2,200W inverter at part load burns about an eighth of a gallon an hour — roughly 8 hours per gallon — so two five-gallon cans cover a three-day outage. An 8,000W open-frame machine would take 20 to 30 gallons.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Small generator or battery power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A battery is silent, runs indoors and needs no fuel — ideal for flats, medical equipment and outages of hours. Its capacity is a fixed budget only mains or solar refills. An engine runs as long as you feed it, which is what a multi-day storm needs. Outdoor space and long outages favour the engine.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">A Realistic Small-Generator Outage Kit</h2>
        <p>
          The machine is about two-thirds of what makes this work. The rest is a small box kept beside it, so that nothing has to be found in the dark: two heavy-gauge outdoor extension cords, a sealed window cord pass, a funnel, a head torch with fresh batteries, a quart of the correct oil, and the manual. Assembled once, it turns deployment into a five-minute job rather than a scavenger hunt through a dark garage.
        </p>
        <p>
          Add two five-gallon cans of stabilised fuel, dated with a marker and rotated into a car every three to six months. On a small machine that genuinely is three days of power, which is the quiet advantage this whole category has over anything larger — the fuel plan is small enough that people actually keep to it.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes With Small Generators
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Small Means Safe Indoors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 2,200-watt engine produces carbon monoxide in lethal amounts just as a 12,000-watt one does. Twenty feet from every window, door and vent, never in a garage with the door open, never on a balcony. Only a battery power station runs inside.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Peak Watts Instead of Running Watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The larger number on the box is a surge rating available for a second or two. A "2,200-watt" generator produces 1,800 continuously, and that is the figure your refrigerator and furnace live on. Size against running watts and treat peak as headroom for motor starts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting Everything at Once</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Small generators have little surge margin, so two compressors starting together will trip one. Plug things in and start them one at a time, heaviest first, and let each settle. It is a habit worth practising before you need it in the dark.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underestimating a Well or Sump Pump</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A well pump surges to 3,000-4,000 watts, beyond every machine here. If the house depends on one for water — or on a sump pump to stay dry in a storm — a small generator is the wrong tool, and no load management fixes it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting It Sit With Untreated Fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Light machines gum up exactly like heavy ones, and a varnished carburetor is what stops a stored generator starting. Exercise it monthly under load, and store it either full of stabilised fuel or with the bowl run completely dry.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

