          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The <strong class="text-white">best small generator for home use</strong> is the <strong class="text-white">Honda EU2200i</strong> — quiet enough to live beside, light enough that anyone in the house can deploy it, and reliable enough that it starts when it matters. If you need the full essentials package rather than a fridge and lights, the <strong class="text-white">Westinghouse iGen4500</strong>; for value, the <strong class="text-white">Champion 2500</strong>; for a flat, the <strong class="text-white">EcoFlow Delta 2</strong>.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Be clear about the ceiling before you buy. 1,800 watts is a refrigerator, lights and devices; 3,000 to 3,700 adds a freezer, a furnace and a small sump pump — which keeps a house warm, dry and stocked. <strong class="text-white">No small generator starts a well pump or runs central air</strong>, and if either is essential you are shopping in the wrong category.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Within those limits, small is not a compromise. It is lighter to deploy, cheap to fuel across a three-day outage, quiet enough to keep the neighbours, and it needs no electrician — which is why it is the generator that actually gets used.
